{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1556054-essences-avatars-and-the-portrayal-of-characters","id":1556054,"title":"Essences, Avatars and The Portrayal of Characters","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-12-01T06:18:50-08:00","id":1556054,"title":"Essences, Avatars and The Portrayal of Characters","content":"Lately, I have been reading Mage: The Ascension books for the first time and I wanted to start making some characters for a campaign, but I had some questions about how Essences really work and how to properly diferentiate a Dynamic Mage from a Questing Mage in their portrayal. I got confused while reading the examples of possible Avatars in <b>Gods and Monsters</b> and past sourcebooks didn't clear my doubts in the topic.<br /> <br /> In the <b>M20 Corebook</b>, p.266-267, there's a quick comparison before the book develops each essence: <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A Dynamic Essence Mage feels restless, driven, impassioned, and hyperactive; his Pattern companion might be more settled, reliable, one of those good head on her shoulders types with a solid approach toward life. A Questing vagabond rarely sticks around one place for long, and the enigmatic Primordial soul gazes at her surroudings with eerie calm and an agenda no one else can fathom. </div> </div> </div> Now, here's my first question: a Questing Mage could also be restless in pursuit of the next mountain to climb; a Pattern Mage could be in his precise demeanor as eerie as Primordial Mage; and a Dynamic Mage could be passionate about mysteries as described in the paragraph of the Primordial Essence in p.267, so <i>what key differences is there in the Essences to better contrast them with each other</i>?<br /> <br /> Besides that, there's the metaphysical aspect of Mage (and Werewolf) to account for. Dynamism related to the Marauders and the Wyld, Pattern to the Technocracy and the Weaver, Primordial to the Nephandi and the Wyrm, and Questing to the Traditions and Balance. Admitedly, the M20 casts that the aforementioned connection is broad, but still, can a Garou, for example, identify the &quot;Taint of the Wyrm&quot; in a Primordial Mage? Morever, isn't there different aspects to each of those metaphysical concepts, like the distinction between a Defiler Wyrm and a Balance Wyrm. How those differences affect the narratives of Mage, if at all?<br /> <br /> The Avatars presented in <b>Gods and Monsters</b>, p.124-127, don't really help with answering my questions. The Manic Pixie (Questing Avatar) seems to me to be an Avatar intent on pushing the mage to see this &quot;dynamic&quot; aspect of life, while Mask and Stalks-The-Master (both Primordial Avatars) are both Avatars that seem to push the mage beyond the boundaries of timidity and fear respectively, which makes me think that the mage that would have those Avatars is not &quot;eerie&quot; per se, but someone whom their lack of confidence keeps them from their path to Ascension.<br /> <br /> Lastly, sourcebooks from previous editions seem to hold quite a distinct view of Essences and how they define Avatars. In <b>Guide to The Technocracy</b>, p.136-137, the essence of a Genius relates more to the position of a given character has with how Enlighntened Science must be applied in the world, with a Dynamic Genius being proactive, Pattern being a slow foundation, Primordial seeking to see beyond the limitations of that scientific foundation, and Questing seeking to be the vanguard of knowledge. <b>Guide to The Traditions</b> takes a different approach and associate paradigm, Essence and a given sphere to shape the worldview of a character, but the post is already verbose so I will not develop that.<br /> <br /> My main question in the end is: <u>what really distinguishes the Essences of characters <i>narratively</i>, more so than mechanically or lore-wise?</u> Examples would be really helpful.<br /> <br /> (Apologies for the length of the post and possible errros in grammar and formatting, since this is my first post in the forum and english is not my first language)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28986-agrat","name":"Agrat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28986_1.webp","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-01T06:50:00-08:00","id":1556056,"title":"","content":"Don't worry about grammar, the worst posters in this regard are all native English speakers, and something between a third and a half of the forum are not.<br /> <br /> There's nothing to your question, really. Essences are just a late-2nd/Revised attempt to rewrite the previous Resonance trait in a way that conforms more to Werewolf's cosmology for reasons, but don't really deal with any core assumption of the game. Which is a problem Resonance already had for the entirety of the game's existence.<br /> <br /> Mostly they exist to flavor Magic and Quintessence, nothing else, and even that is in a rather loose manner. They have exactly the impact you want them to have in the game, nothing more or less.<br /> <br /> Avatar classifications are likewise arbitrary. The point of an Avatar is that they nudge the Mage towards Ascension in some manner, for whatever interpretation of &quot;nudging&quot;, &quot;Ascension&quot; and &quot;manner&quot; you want. Any classification misses the point more than helps, and Essences are no exception.<br /> <br /> Garou don't usually identify triatic taint from a Mage because of Essence, unless thats the effect you want for a given character or your entire chronicle. Even their link to the &quot;factions&quot;, or to Quiet, are later revisions and you don't really need 'em.<br /> <br /> So, at the end of the day the answer to your question is &quot;whatever you want&quot;, because there's no canon answer as this is a concept thrown in in the books with very little context or explanation and are no more than rough ideas.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-01T07:56:52-08:00","id":1556060,"title":"","content":"Essence exists since 1rst Edition, it wasn't &quot;thrown in in the books&quot; any more than most of the core concepts of the game, such as &quot;Ascencion&quot;.<br /> <br /> It relates a bit to Ressonance, maybe, but this really isn't a thing until Revised. And I think it just added a layer of complexity that wasn't needed to explain Essence.<br /> <br /> It HAS a lot to do with Werewolf because Mage since the 1rst edition had a lot to do with Werewolf and Vampire, and it has tried to sepparate since then, but the roots of the game are what they are. And it has not been consistent about this.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Agrat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Mask and Stalks-The-Master (both Primordial Avatars) are both Avatars that seem to push the mage beyond the boundaries of timidity and fear respectively, which makes me think that the mage that would have those Avatars is not &quot;eerie&quot; per se, but someone whom their lack of confidence keeps them from their path to Ascension.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think there may be a core misconception here:<br /> <br /> The Essence isn't describing the personality or identity of the character - <b>that would be Nature and Demeanor</b>. The Essence it's more like Nature &amp; Demeanor <i>of the Avatar</i><br /> <br /> Mages are influenced by their Avatar in certain ways - they're being &quot;pushed&quot;, as you say. This does influence them and the way they act. It also can colour their magick, arguably, albeit this was never seriously tackled until Revised, that tied Ressonance, Essence and the Trinity. And then Synergy. But that's a load of stuff that really it's diverting from the question at hand:<br /> <br /> <b>Essence it's the &quot;Nature&quot; of the Avatar</b><br /> <br /> On 1rst edition it was said &quot;Nature and Demeanor defines your character in human terms, but Essence defines that aspect of your character that makes her a mage&quot;<br /> <br /> So, Essence its a bit like a &quot;magical nature&quot;, that represents your Avatar &quot;pushing&quot; the mage towards Ascencion.<br /> <br /> *Mechanically, an Avatar whose Essence it's ignored may impose a penalty on the Mage when he casts magick (there's a penalty about angering your Avatar on the list of bonuses/penalties)<br /> <br /> *Narratively, it sort of informs your &quot;path&quot; as a Mage. On the Character it would manifest as a sort of &quot;<i>second Demeanor</i>&quot;<br /> <br /> A &quot;timid mage&quot; (with a &quot;timid&quot; Nature &amp; Demeanor) may be pushed towards looking &quot;eery&quot;, by an &quot;eery&quot; Avatar. Even when he wasn't, and may not even <i>like</i> it. It also makes sense for the Scientists to pursue avenues of research that would &quot;accomodate&quot; his Genious.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Relation to Werewolf Cosmology</b><br /> <br /> This is always going to be iffy, because the cosmologies never meshed.<br /> <br /> But, in essence, <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> , Mage contemplates the &quot;Metaphisical Trinity&quot; of Entropy, Dynamism and Stasis. These are fundamental forces or principles, not spirits - The Wyrm, Wyld and Weaver are the spirits that represent these forces or principles.<br /> <br /> Each of the &quot;extreme&quot; Essences it's linked/related to one of the <b><i>Trinity</i> </b>(Not the Triad! , <b>Avatars aren't triatic spirits! </b>- well, not usually at least). All things on Creation are influenced by the Trinity/Triad to some extent, but not all things are triatic spirits, nor &quot;tainted&quot;. Essences being related to the forces doesn't mean they're an extension of the spirits that represent the forces.<br /> <br /> Now,<i> the link exists</i> (even if it's<i> very small</i>), and it may influence mages on Werewolf crossovers to some extent. For instance, Stasis mages may be more prone to &quot;hearing the One Song&quot; and becoming a Drone than Dynamic ones. But it's just a very minor tendency. Technocracy has the largest amount of Drones according to the book of the Weaver, yet, according to the same book, the Technocracy isn't &quot;of the Weaver&quot;, and the Weaver doesn't like Mages in general because they warp reality - <i>she's more related to Paradox than to any mage of any essence</i>.<br /> <br /> This is not enough to start smelling like anything. Not more than a sandwich with too many additives, or a regular dude that works at a &quot;droning&quot; office job, anyway.<br /> <br /> Nephandi and other people that are so unbalanced to one extreme may start &quot;smelling&quot;, depending on the ST and the book. But I don't they should, really. This should be a function of having a super loaded <b>Ressonance</b> <br /> <br /> <b>Relation to Ressonance/Synergy</b><br /> <br /> Essence supposedly describes also how you do magick. I would say this is mostly trough the Avatar imposing penalties if you act against it's &quot;Nature&quot;, and suggesting courses of action that are according to it.<br /> <br /> When Mages act uppon the world with Magick, their actions cause Ressonance. A sort of small effect magick has over you. Mages that soothe and heal people may start having a calming influence over people. Mages that kill a lot with magick start smelling like death has come. That's <b>Ressonance</b><br /> <br /> If we had to link <b>Ressonance </b>narratively to any other game, I would say that it's the closest Mage has to a Humanity rating. The way you use magick reflects on how your mage, and their action, looks to the outside world. It doesn't ever require you to retire your character (except for <b><i>Jhor</i></b>, maybe) but it sort of acts as a consequence for your actions<br /> <br /> It makes sense to think that a strongly Triatic Avatar it's going to suggest Triatic-aligned actions. That being said, most Avatars aren't that triatic to begin with. Nephandi have very special Avatars, and even then not all serve the Wyrm.<br /> <br /> On Revised it was determined to honor the link each Essence has to one member of the Trinity by forcing each type of Ressonance. This never really worked, but was salvaged on M20 as Synergy. And, as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> said, there's not much more to say about it.<br /> <br /> Anyway, if anything it's going to make a Mage &quot;smell like the Wyrm&quot;, it would be having a Ressonance by<i> doing lots of Wyrm-related things with magick </i>(such as summoning Banes, for instance).<br /> <br /> And, no, having a Primordial ressonance isn't enough. Not even &quot;enacting&quot; it. At most you could say a Primordial Avatar it's sligthly more likely to show their mage courses of action that would end smelling (or at least wouldn't actively discourage them). But I would say having a cruel Nature/Demeanor combo (such as Monster or another cynical nature) it's going to be more conducent to end with your magick &quot;smelling the wyrm&quot;, than having an &quot;eery&quot; Avatar. And in any case, it should always be uppon the character's &quot;actions&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":".  corrections 12-01-2025, 11:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-01T08:35:29-08:00","id":1556062,"title":"","content":"<b>About the Questing / Dynamic thing.</b><br /> <br /> This one took me back, I remember to ask the same question when I started Mage long ago.<br /> <br /> As I had it explained to me, the difference it's small, but substantial. It's the difference between a guy that always starts a new project before finishing the last, and a guy that's always seeking for new experiences, but commits to them.<br /> <br /> The Questing Avatar it's going to push you to the next mountain top, but after you've reached the last one. The Dynamic Avatar it's always going to show you a new course of action even if you're not done with the current one.<br /> <br /> But, remember, this is the <i>Avatar</i>. This is going to colour the Ascencion path, the seekings, other interactions with the Avatar, maybe the Ressonance if you chose to play with Synergy.<br /> But the <i>mage </i>has to choose how to deal with it as a human being, and because of that they may not always reflect this trait. It can be fun to play with a mage whose character it's conflictive with essence, and has to work on some &quot;Magic(k)/Life balance&quot; of sorts. Most mages <i>do </i>reflect their Essence, it's the path of least resistance after all","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-01T08:36:00-08:00","id":1556063,"title":"","content":"This isn't anything canon, but if it helps you to outline the traits of Essence a bit better, you can extrapolate by association (correspondence) of the number four:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table align=\"left\" class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Dynamic</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Pattern</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Primordial</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Questing</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Fire</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Earth</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Air</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Water</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Spring</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Summer</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Winter</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Autumn</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Choleric</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Melancholic</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Sanguine</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Phlegmatic</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Spirit</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Body</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Soul</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Mind</td> </tr> </table></div> <br /> ..and so on. This would be very much a view on the Essences from a Hermetic paradigm, but doing this helps me whenever I need to fit something fundamental into the larger picture. How much these traits inform the narrative of a character is of course up to the player, but as keywords they may be useful for a general orientation.<br /> <br /> I personally think Questing is an awkward fit when the other Essences already make a direct correspondence to the Triat, but that's just my need for pleasing structures.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":27477,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","user":"voidshaper","date":". 12-01-2025, 11:39 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-01T13:49:38-08:00","id":1556067,"title":"","content":"Similar to Aleph, the way I explain the &quot;balance&quot; concept of Questing is thus:<br /> <br /> Questing Essence mages start projects with the energy and passion of a Dynamic mage, pursue their goals with the focus of a Static mage, and reflect on their experiences with the mentality of a Primordial mage. Basically, every thing they start is a quest through Mage's triad of fundamental natural forces as discussed above; moving through the cycle instead of being focused on one part of it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-01T16:38:32-08:00","id":1556072,"title":"","content":"Thank you for the answers, they really helped me to see the distinction between the Essences.<br /> <br /> I would like to extend the question a bit, since we are already talking about the relationship between an Avatar and its Mage, and another post for this one seems unnecessary. There seems to be a purposeful vagueness to what the Avatar really <i>is</i> in the game (or at least I think it's on purpose and not the consequence of merely a lot of people writing Mage over the years). In some cases, it's treated as some kind of &quot;other soul&quot;, a direct reflection of the Mage; in others, the Avatar is considered a distinct and autonomous entity from the Mage. As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1683\" data-vbnamecard=\"1683\">Aleph</a> said, the intent of an Avatar can change its Mage without even the Mage's consideration, this is exemplified by the existence of widderslaintes, which didn't choose a Nephandic paradigm to begin with and needs to go against their inverted Avatar if said widderslainte wants to not be evil. Things can get really weird if we take the concept of an Avatar as a reflection of the Mage and create a character that uses in conjunction the Merits Avatar Companion, Manifest Avatar and True Love (a combination that the Book of Secrets alludes to be possible), is the Mage here loving himself in a unhealthy-narcisistic-power-trip in order to Ascend?<br /> <br /> Admitedly, the answer of &quot;<i>What is the nature of Avatar?</i>&quot; is clearly something to be defined at the end of the day by the Storyteller. Even then, I wanted to ask: how do you choose to define Avatars in your games and why did you choose that specific way?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28986-agrat","name":"Agrat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28986_1.webp","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-01T18:05:09-08:00","id":1556074,"title":"","content":"Funnily enough, Mage has had the most consistent authorial lead of the original five WoD games, with Brucato writing a bunch of the 1e supplements before being the lead developer for all of 2e (including Sorcerer's Crusade) and M20.<br /> <br /> So, part of the confusion about Avatars is a purposeful in-universe thing that happened as the setting moved from the mythical ages, to the SC era, and then into the modern times. The earliest mages - and thus the founders of the philosophic groups that would lead to the different Fellowships, Traditions, Crafts, Disparates, and Conventions - were in-unity with their Avatars by default to the point where they didn't even know that Avatars were a &quot;thing,&quot; because Avatars couldn't manifest or express themselves as anything more than stray thoughts in the mages' minds. So for thousands of years, mages this inner power was interpreted by the local beliefs that mages grew up with. Reincarnations, divine sparks, attunement with other planes of existence, etc. were what mages were taught by older mages for generations. Then something happened, and the matching of Avatars and mages, starting getting imperfect. As time passed, mages became more and more aware of their Avatars as not just part of their spiritual natures as humans awakened to magical reality, and tried to mesh this with their traditional teaches from before this. And of course, being mages that can warp reality, Avatars tried to adapt to their mages' belief systems; taking on the attributes that older mages gave them before this divide started. As time passed though, the divide continued to grow and the disparity between Avatar and mage grew to the point where Avatars became fully independent entities filtered by their mages' perspectives loosely.<br /> <br /> That doesn't really explain &quot;what&quot; an Avatar truly is in Mage, but it can help put context to why mages themselves are confused on the matter and why modern Avatars can vary so wildly between ones that still have some form of their older nature and others have fully adapted to modern times.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-01T20:04:56-08:00","id":1556080,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1556054-essences-avatars-and-the-portrayal-of-characters?p=1556060#post1556060\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now,<i> the link exists</i> (even if it's<i> very small</i>), and it may influence mages on Werewolf crossovers to some extent. For instance, Stasis mages may be more prone to &quot;hearing the One Song&quot; and becoming a Drone than Dynamic ones. But it's just a very minor tendency. Technocracy has the largest amount of Drones according to the book of the Weaver, yet, according to the same book, the Technocracy isn't &quot;of the Weaver&quot;, and the Weaver doesn't like Mages in general because they warp reality - <i>she's more related to Paradox than to any mage of any essence</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's relevant to notice that even for the <b><i>Trinity</i></b>, and even more to the Triat, Mages are linked to each of them just in relative terms. Overall they're more <i>Dynamic</i> than most other supernaturals with the possible exception of Changelings. The extent of this varies, as this is not depicted consistently, but overall being strongly anything other than Dynamic is almost impossible for a Mage. They're restless, with a head full of ideas about the world they want and the power to make that world come to be. Depending on the book, becoming too dissociated from Dynamism may even be linked to situations that make them stop being Mages (keeping in mind that not everyone at the Technocracy are Enlightened personnel).<br /> <br /> Now, it's important for Mage that this <i>relativity</i> is meaningful. A Pattern Mage is anything but static, but is still a force for Stasis, or for some aspect/vision of Stasis. Yet both things are real and important.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Agrat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1556054-essences-avatars-and-the-portrayal-of-characters?p=1556072#post1556072\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There seems to be a purposeful vagueness to what the Avatar really <i>is</i> in the game (or at least I think it's on purpose and not the consequence of merely a lot of people writing Mage over the years).</div> </div> </div> </div>For a number of things in the game, including Avatars, Resonance and Essences, the vagueness is by design, as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/\">Heavy Arms</a> said. Very few things in Mage are just the result of too many hands, it's quite consistent in this regard, so most inconsistencies are there on purpose.<br /> <br /> So we don't really know what an Avatar is and that's the point. The best approach is to not define them, but just let each case be whatever will be more interesting. Avatars have a lot of abilities and powers that may put them among the most powerful entities in existence. But they may also be just some kind of hallucination, and anything in between. It's even possible that different Avatars are different things entirely except for also being Avatars,\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-02T08:49:00-08:00","id":1556097,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1556054-essences-avatars-and-the-portrayal-of-characters?p=1556080#post1556080\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now, it's important for Mage that this <i>relativity</i> is meaningful. A Pattern Mage is anything but static, but is still a force for Stasis, or for some aspect/vision of Stasis. Yet both things are real and important.</div> </div> </div> </div>Aye. Everything it's <i>connected </i>, but that's not to say they're the same thing.<br /> <br /> Maybe one could see it in a similar vein as how there are Fire Elementals that are Gaian, a corrupted version of the Wyrm (Balefire), and then there are whole Fire Eemental Courts on the Astral/High Umbra that aren't Gaian, nor of the Wyrm , but rather are closer (on Werewolf terms) to Epiphlings. One can bet all of them care about Fire, but in different ways.<br /> <br /> A Mage's magick it's always Dynamic. Yet on the Book of the Wyld, its said the Wyld doesn't claim mages either - because mages <i>shape </i>reality.<br /> <br /> If we interpret magic(k) as changing the true-name of things (understanding mages trough the &quot;name-breaker&quot; angle), and Naming it's the purview of the Weaver as she stabilizes the Wyld's creation. Mage's breaking reality into a new shape are re-naming stuff. The Weaver feels challenged, yet the Wyld doesn't gain much.<br /> <br /> A <i>possible </i>exception are Marauders. On older books they were strongly related to the Wyld, and the Book of the Wyld vaguely acknowledged this. Yet, from Revised edition, and the more recent division of Quiet, I would say this is very doubtful. A Negation Man seems much more Weaver-ish than Wyld. If any Marauder could stll retain this old &quot;connection&quot; with the Wyld, it would be Marauders with a Dynamic Quiet: Madness ... And Dynamic Essences certainly are more <i>prone </i>to falling into Madness, so, there's that. But they're not the only ones, Questing mages would fall too, and nothing says mages of the other &quot;extreme&quot; Essences can't (but it would be much rarer, as such Avatar are likely to fight a too Dynamic path).<br /> <br /> In a similar vein, a Pattern Mage it's guided by some aspect/vision of Stasis. But that doesn't make it &quot;of the Weaver&quot;. There's an <i>affinity</i> maybe. As a force for Statism recognizes another. All similar things in the WoD are connected. And that could lead to falling to the Weaver. But that's only if the mage takes a path that leads there.<br /> <br /> Ultimaltely, mages are &quot;human&quot;. But they are influenced by cosmic forces, and that's a good part of what makes mages different from even other human magic users. Maybe it's not as important, mechanically, as having Dynamic Magic(k). But narratively, normal people don't have a &quot;secondary personality&quot; trait added to Nature&amp;Demeanor. Mages DO change when they Awaken. And they only change more as they progress: have Seekings to evolve your magick, accumulate Ressonance by casting spells aligned to your Avatar, and it's influence over the mage it's almost bound to become more prevalent.<br /> <br /> I think this also colours why it's so traumatic to be Gilguled, or to become a Vampire. Imagine having the creativity of a Dynamic Essence, the sense of purpose of a Questing mage, the stability of a Static Essence, or the deep insigth of a Primordial mage. And then, poof, it's gone. Anyone would be depressed if a huge <i>drive </i>like that get's removed so suddenly ... Sure, loosing the Spheres it's also a boomer - that's a lot of power to loose (even if you win to live forever in exchange). But I think loosing the Essence helps better to colour the &quot;crash&quot;, narratively.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Agrat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Admitedly, the answer of &quot;<i>What is the nature of Avatar?</i>&quot; is clearly something to be defined at the end of the day by the Storyteller. Even then, I wanted to ask: how do you choose to define Avatars in your games and why did you choose that specific way?</div> </div> </div> </div>Lore-wise, I go with them being &quot;shards of the One&quot;, that are part of &quot;the soul&quot;, and they detach after death as the soul get's divided in it's constitutent parts (taking a page from Mummy), and this part in particular goes back to the &quot;wheel of souls&quot; on the Astral Umbra to reincarnate.<br /> <br /> But theres a lot of loaded words there that can be interpreted several ways (what is &quot;the One&quot;?, what does it mean to be a shard of it? , what is &quot;the soul&quot;?). I.E:<br /> <br /> *The soul could be the psyche (it's actually the same word <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ), or you as a &quot;human being&quot; that exist in a society and a history.<br /> <br /> *The One could be &quot;Nature&quot; as defined by the Antrophic Principle (thank you Void Engineers ! ), and each individual human it's part of it (the universe developping observers it's the only observable universe - the One it's just the fact &quot;the universe&quot; or &quot;Nature&quot; it's the sum of our observations about it).<br /> <br /> *You need <i>DimSci </i>to &quot;Gilgul&quot; because the &quot;Genius&quot; it's part of the same phenomena that DimSci studies as the Antrophic Principle. Being a Genius isn't just &quot;being very intelligent&quot; (that's high Intelligence), but your relationship with the current world as someone who can shape it. Or, as <b>Jimmy Neutron </b>would put it: &quot; <b>what is genius but an artificial construct in the guise of an empirical truth?</b>&quot;<br /> <br /> ....<br /> <br /> Those questions are better left unanswered. Rather, <b>I would ask the player</b> how do their character defines <i>Avatar</i>. And try to roll with it:<br /> <br /> *It may well be that the Avatar it's an avatar of an Incarna, for instance, a spirit belonging to the Umbra that attached itself to the mage. In that case I would say the Umbra would react accordingly (i.e. having Odin as Avatar it's going to be aknowledged by Odin, the God - even if this isn't always recognized by it's &quot;brood&quot;, I think your relationship to it should matter).<br /> <br /> It still get's detached after death, passes trough the Astral Umbra, and maybe goes back to it's spirit patron before reincarnating (and who's to say Incarna aren't shards of the One?).<br /> <br /> *It could also be, as you said: &quot;a reflection of the Mage&quot;. &quot;a character that uses in conjunction the Merits Avatar Companion, Manifest Avatar and True Love (a combination that the Book of Secrets alludes to be possible) ... its loving himself in a unhealthy-narcisistic-power-trip in order to Ascend?&quot;. Sounds like a fun concept to me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 12-02-2025, 11:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-03T14:35:35-08:00","id":1556136,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1556054-essences-avatars-and-the-portrayal-of-characters?p=1556097#post1556097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A <i>possible </i>exception are Marauders.</div> </div> </div> </div>I personally think <i>Revised</i> did a good work with them. Their original depictions had many, many problems, from a too limited narrative focus (they're all Crypto-Eco Terrorists) to being a really stereotypical depiction of mind pathology. Sure, Quite and Maraudom aren't really mental sickness, they're Magical Madness, neither are other Mages right in their minds, but still it needed work. And <i>Revised </i>mostly delivered.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1556054-essences-avatars-and-the-portrayal-of-characters?p=1556097#post1556097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ultimaltely, mages are &quot;human&quot;. But they are influenced by cosmic forces, and that's a good part of what makes mages different from even other human magic users.<br /> ...<br /> Anyone would be depressed if a huge <i>drive </i>like that get's removed so suddenly\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>That's one of the keys to understand Mages. They have <i>drive</i>, they have drive to a supernatural degree. They are given Vision for the world and the power to make it real.<br /> <br /> We are literally talking people that can't be proven wrong, because no matter what they think, they can prove it. And they're driven to elaborate further and further. They can't agree for long on every detail, they can't accept the world as is (although they may believe it in a philosophical level, for a given value of each of those words), they can't just accept authority for real on matters of belief.<br /> <br /> Even Agents of the Union struggle with this. They're exceedingly good at <i>pretending</i> to be conformists, yet they're anything but, at least the Enlightened ones.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1556054-essences-avatars-and-the-portrayal-of-characters?p=1556097#post1556097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Those questions are better left unanswered. Rather, <b>I would ask the player</b> how do their character defines <i>Avatar</i>. And try to roll with it</div> </div> </div> </div>At the end that applies to all those things that are left vague by the books.<br /> <br /> Essence, Avatars, Resonance, the Trinity.. if anything seems vague to the ST in matters of what it means in the setting, then getting the PC's answers is far more important than defining a definitive truth.<br /> <br /> Mage is not about definitive truths, it's about people that make their personal truths real to a concrete level. The opinions of those people will literally shape reality, so don't waste an opportunity of exploring their thoughts on it.<br /> <br /> We have also to remember that a lot of things in Mage aren't exactly objective truth, but a Paradigmatic truth. Attributing the Trinity to the &quot;factions&quot; is a Tradition thought, as is a lot of explanations for rules big and small. Literally, theories that have identifiable origins through the setting's history and are debated by the characters in the fiction.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-12T03:48:37-08:00","id":1556509,"title":"","content":"Personally, I prefer to downplay or even outright disregard the Essence/Triat connection. I view Primordial as being more along the overall lines of <b>Werewolf</b> in general: primitive, wild, and untamed; and rather than Questing being specifically about &quot;balancing the Triat,&quot; I think of it as someone who is fundamentally goal-oriented, always working to further some cause or higher ideal. <br /> <br /> I <i>do</i> connect the Essences to the Resonance flavors, but indirectly: an Avatar with a Pattern Essence will provide guidance that, when followed, results in the mage taking actions that tend to generate Stabilizing Resonance; a Questing Avatar doesn't cause Devotional Resonance, but a mage guided by one tends to take actions that generate Devotional Resonance; Dynamic Avatars similarly promote, but do not cause, Temperamental Resonance; and Primordial Avatars guide their mages in ways that lead to Elemental Resonance. But ultimately, the Resonance that a mage generates derives from how the mage uses Magick, not from what the mage's Avatar Essence is. <br /> <br /> I also use the Resonances to flavor Quiet; and I <i>don't </i>use the model of there being three distinct types of Quiet. Rather, any Quiet will manifest in three ways, often all at the same time: delusions (seeing things that aren't there), denials (<i>not</i> seeing things that <i>are</i> there), and exaggerations (overemphasizing aspects of what's there). Some tend to lean into one of these aspects more than the others; for instance, the Negative Men (the closest thing <b>Mage</b> has to <b>Changeling</b>'s Autumn People) have a powerful Stabilizing Resonance that manifests almost exclusively as denial of anything wondrous. What M20 calls Morbidity is a perfectly ordinary Quiet that's heavily influenced by the Elemental Resonance of Death, causing the Quiet to exaggerate decay and sickly pallor in everything around it. And so on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-12T08:35:12-08:00","id":1556516,"title":"","content":"I also tend to downplay the connection between the Essences and the Triat. However, mages who have Primordial Essences often have cosmology views that tend to be closely aligned with those of werewolves and other Changing Breeds.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-12T17:20:21-08:00","id":1556543,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1556054-essences-avatars-and-the-portrayal-of-characters?p=1556509#post1556509\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">the Negative Men (the closest thing <b>Mage</b> has to <b>Changeling</b>'s Autumn People)</div> </div> </div> </div>It just occurred to me that a Negative Man could be actually quite Magical and disruptive in anything that's not strictly the most mundane Reality Zone, or not whatever Reality they deem normal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-14T01:52:27-08:00","id":1556598,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1556054-essences-avatars-and-the-portrayal-of-characters?p=1556516#post1556516\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also tend to downplay the connection between the Essences and the Triat. However, mages who have Primordial Essences often have cosmology views that tend to be closely aligned with those of werewolves and other Changing Breeds.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div